"""Plain-language glossary of materials-simulation terms."""

GLOSSARY: dict[str, str] = {
    "DFT (density functional theory)": "A quantum-mechanical method that computes the energy of a set of atoms from their electron density. Accurate but expensive: hours to days on a cluster.",
    "VASP": "The Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package, a widely used (licensed) DFT program. This app prepares its input files and reads its outputs; it never runs VASP.",
    "POSCAR": "VASP's structure file: lattice vectors, element names and counts, and atom positions. `CONTCAR` is the same format written after a relaxation.",
    "INCAR": "VASP's settings file (cut-off energy, smearing, relaxation options…).",
    "KPOINTS": "How finely VASP samples reciprocal space (the Brillouin zone). Denser = more accurate and slower. Metals need denser meshes.",
    "POTCAR": "Pseudopotential files describing each element's core electrons. Licensed by the VASP group, so the app never displays or exports them.",
    "ENCUT": "Plane-wave energy cut-off (eV). Higher = more accurate basis, slower. Found with a convergence test.",
    "Convergence test": "Repeating a calculation while increasing ENCUT or k-point density until the energy changes by less than ~1 meV/atom.",
    "Relaxation": "Moving atoms (and optionally the cell) until forces vanish, i.e. finding the nearest stable arrangement.",
    "EOS (equation of state)": "Energy as a function of volume, E(V). Its minimum gives the equilibrium volume V₀; its curvature gives the bulk modulus B₀ (stiffness under pressure).",
    "Bulk modulus (B, K)": "Resistance to uniform compression, in GPa. Diamond ≈ 440 GPa, aluminium ≈ 76 GPa.",
    "Elastic constants (Cᵢⱼ)": "A 6×6 matrix relating small strains to stresses. From it come bulk (K), shear (G) and Young's (E) moduli and Poisson's ratio.",
    "Voigt / Reuss / Hill": "Upper bound, lower bound and their average for the moduli of a polycrystal computed from single-crystal Cᵢⱼ.",
    "AIMD": "Ab-initio molecular dynamics: atoms move according to DFT forces at a temperature. Used for diffusion and finite-temperature behaviour.",
    "MSD (mean squared displacement)": "Average squared distance atoms have travelled. Its slope over time gives the diffusion coefficient D = slope / 6.",
    "Arrhenius plot / activation energy": "log D vs 1/T is a straight line whose slope gives the activation energy of diffusion.",
    "NEB (nudged elastic band)": "Finds the minimum-energy path and energy barrier between two structures (e.g. an atom hopping into a vacancy). 'Climbing image' pins the saddle point.",
    "MLP (machine-learning potential)": "A neural network trained on DFT data that predicts energies and forces in milliseconds. SevenNet, CHGNet, Orb-v3 and MatterSim are 'universal' MLPs covering most of the periodic table.",
    "Single point": "One energy evaluation of a structure; in Masgent's MLP tool, atom positions are also relaxed (fixed cell) with LBFGS.",
    "fmax": "Largest remaining force on any atom (eV/Å). A relaxation stops when fmax falls below the tolerance.",
    "NVT / Nosé–Hoover": "Molecular dynamics at constant number of atoms, volume and temperature, using a Nosé–Hoover thermostat.",
    "Supercell": "A larger cell made of several copies of the unit cell. Needed so that defects or vibrations do not interact with their periodic images.",
    "Vacancy / substitution / interstitial": "Point defects: a missing atom, an atom replaced by another element, or an extra atom squeezed into a void.",
    "Voronoi interstitial sites": "Candidate voids found from the Voronoi tessellation of the crystal: the points farthest from surrounding atoms.",
    "SQS (special quasirandom structure)": "A small periodic cell whose atom arrangement mimics a perfectly random alloy as closely as possible (built with icet).",
    "Miller indices (hkl)": "Notation for crystal planes. (111) in an fcc metal is the close-packed plane.",
    "Slab / vacuum": "A few atomic layers of a crystal with empty space above to model a surface.",
    "Interface / lattice mismatch": "Two crystals joined along planes. Their in-plane lattice vectors must nearly match; the mismatch (%) causes strain.",
    "Energy above hull": "How far (eV/atom) a compound lies above the most stable combination of phases. 0 = thermodynamically stable.",
    "Space group": "The symmetry of a crystal (230 possibilities), e.g. Fm-3m for fcc metals.",
    "Materials Project (MP)": "A free online database of DFT-computed materials. Needs a free API key.",
    "Slurm": "The job scheduler used on most HPC clusters. `sbatch script.sh` submits a job.",
    "PCA": "Principal component analysis: compresses many correlated features into a few uncorrelated ones.",
    "CVAE": "Conditional variational auto-encoder: a generative network used here to synthesise extra training samples.",
    "Optuna": "A hyper-parameter optimisation library; Masgent uses it to search neural-network architectures with cross-validation.",
    "Cross-validation (k-fold)": "Training k times on different 80 % splits and validating on the remaining 20 % to estimate generalisation.",
    "RMSE / R²": "Root-mean-square error (in the target's units; lower is better) and coefficient of determination (1 = perfect).",
    "Parity plot": "Predicted vs true values; points on the diagonal are perfect predictions.",
    "CALPHAD": "Thermodynamic modelling of phase diagrams; the Al-Mg-Si-Sc predictor uses CALPHAD phase fractions as features.",
    "HEA (high-entropy alloy)": "An alloy of five or more elements in similar amounts, e.g. AlCoCrFeNi.",
}
